course: dictionary
lesson: 12924
title: Evacuees and moving to safety
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An evacuee is a person who has been moved away from a dangerous place to somewhere safer. The verb is evacuate, which means to leave a dangerous place, and the noun for the whole moving-away is evacuation. Notice the ending: an evacuee is a person, so it is a word for who, not for where.
explain: «The town hall was full of evacuees after the flood.» The clues are flood and full of. The people in the hall had to leave their homes because the water was dangerous, so they are evacuees. Ask yourself: who was moved, and why? That tells you the meaning.
explain: In the Second World War, child evacuees were sent from cities to the countryside to escape bombing. Operation Pied Piper, in 1939, moved many children this way. They often wore a label, carried a gas mask and a suitcase, and stayed with host families.
fact: An evacuee is a person who has been moved away from danger to somewhere safer.
fact: The verb is evacuate, and the noun for the whole process is evacuation.
fact: In the Second World War, child evacuees were sent from British cities to the countryside to escape bombing.
fact: Operation Pied Piper began in 1939 and moved many children out of cities.
fact: Today, people can be evacuees after floods, fires, wars or hurricanes.
exercise: «The evacuees arrived at the station with labels on their coats.» What is an evacuee? | A teacher who writes the school rules | A soldier who guards a castle | *A person who has been moved away from a dangerous place to somewhere safer
exercise: «The family had to evacuate their home before the flood came.» What does evacuate mean? | To build a new house | *To leave a dangerous place | To paint the walls a bright colour
exercise: Which noun names the whole process of moving people to safety? | evacuee | label | host family | *evacuation
test: Why were child evacuees sent to the countryside in 1939? | *To escape bombing in the cities | To visit their grandparents | To learn to farm | To go on a holiday to the seaside
test: Which of these is NOT a reason people might become evacuees today? | A flood | A wildfire | A hurricane | *A birthday party
exam: Which sentence uses evacuee correctly? | Evacuee is a colour of paint. | *The evacuees were given warm blankets in the school hall. | I evacuee my bedroom every morning. | The evacuee baked a cake for the party.
